  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Why this book</h1>
    <p class="muted">Developing argument — evidence case not yet complete</p>
    <p>
      Free enterprise has produced extraordinary material progress. At the same time, many citizens
      experience a widening gap between national productivity and household security; between
      corporate scale and community vitality; between formal political equality and concentrated
      economic power.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Constitutional Capitalism</em> begins from a working diagnosis: lasting prosperity requires
      more than aggregate wealth. It requires institutions that keep markets free, ownership real,
      competition meaningful, and power accountable.
    </p>
    <h2>What is not yet claimed</h2>
    <p>
      This page does not present finished empirical proof. Specific historical claims, tax-incidence
      conclusions, and policy effects will be developed through the research system, claim ledger,
      and later modeling phases. Unfinished claims must remain labeled as unfinished.
    </p>
    <h2>The constitutional question</h2>
    <p>
      If political constitutions restrain government power because power concentrates, what
      comparable discipline should apply when private economic power reaches public scale?
      Answering that question — without abandoning free enterprise — is the work of this book.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
